#!/usr/bin/env python3
# wiki-extension: Watch Confluence pages and tell your wiki when one changes. It can also publish a wiki to a space, if you choose.
# wiki-events: sync tick
# wiki-setup: yes
# wiki-settings: yes
"""wiki confluence: an llm-wiki extension. Standard library only.

It does two separate things, and neither until it is chosen in its settings.

Watching reads Confluence. For the pages a wiki is set to watch, it notes in that wiki
when a page's text changes and when its status changes, with who changed it, when, and
what changed. An open item whose heading links a page closes when that page's status
becomes one that means finished. What has been noted is read back from the wiki's own
notes, so two machines agree. It writes nothing to Confluence.

Publishing writes to Confluence. It copies one wiki at a time, one way: the wiki's
index becomes a page called "<wiki> wiki", and every page under wiki/ becomes a child
of it. Pages it made carry the label llm-wiki and a hash of their source in the version
comment, so an unchanged page isn't written again and a page someone else made is never
overwritten. Nothing is deleted there. With `auto = <wikis>` it publishes those wikis
by itself: on the sync event when a page changed, and on the tick event to catch up.

It uses Confluence's first REST API (/rest/api/content), the only one Data Center has.
Atlassian marks it deprecated on Cloud, where it still answers.
"""
